1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,689
October twenty-second, eighteen seventy-nine.

2
00:00:02,809 --> 00:00:07,239
Edison's lab lights a bulb with a filament of carbonized cotton thread.

3
00:00:07,359 --> 00:00:09,446
It glows for thirteen and a half hours.

4
00:00:09,566 --> 00:00:12,070
The first lamp with real commercial promise.

5
00:00:12,190 --> 00:00:17,201
Eighteen eighty-two: the Pearl Street station in New York starts selling electricity.

6
00:00:17,321 --> 00:00:19,320
About four hundred lamps on day one.

7
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:21,357
Twenty fourteen: a federal limit.

8
00:00:21,477 --> 00:00:25,023
An eight-hundred-lumen bulb can use forty-three watts at most.

9
00:00:25,143 --> 00:00:30,089
Today, an LED gives those same eight hundred lumens for about nine watts.

10
00:00:30,209 --> 00:00:34,625
On three hours a day, that's about one dollar and seventy-nine cents a year,

11
00:00:34,745 --> 00:00:38,083
instead of eleven ninety-five for the old sixty-watt bulb.

12
00:00:38,203 --> 00:00:43,524
All of it started with a cotton thread, on October twenty-second, eighteen seventy-nine…
